+     1	# Literature regression corpus
+     2	
+     3	A small public corpus for measuring how well a literature pipeline reads
+     4	scientific papers. A staged assurance report scores a run of that pipeline
+     5	against an answer key, and keeps each stage's denominator separate. It is the
+     6	public reconstruction of the known-error set in issue 193, part 1. Larger
+     7	sets stay with whoever owns them, and can be scored with the same report.
+     8	
+     9	The documents are **synthetic paraphrases** of public works, written for
+    10	regression testing. Each names its work's public identifier, and each
+    11	identifier was checked against its public record on 2026-11-15. No document
+    12	is the text of its work.
+    13	
+    14	This directory is the entry point, and it holds no labels. The answers are in
+    15	[`answers/`](answers/), kept apart so the corpus can be handed to a reader
+    16	without them.
+    17	
+    18	## What is here
+    19	
+    20	| Path | What it is |
+    21	|---|---|
+    22	| `model.orm.yaml` | The flat corpus model. A method is identified by the name a document uses, as in the host graph the issue reports |
+    23	| `corpus/corpus.json` | The corpus manifest (`literature-corpus/1`): documents, their digests and splits, and every passage |
+    24	| `corpus/documents/*.md` | One document per work: a title line, an identifier line, then one passage per line |
+    25	| `corpus/artifacts/` | `gnd extract` output for the model, which `gnd fidelity` needs |
+    26	| `runs/reference/` | The faithful reference run (`literature-run/1`). It must score exit 0 |
+    27	| `expected/` | The approved expected outcome of each scored case (`literature-assurance-expectation/1`) |
+    28	| `answers/` | The answer key, gold populations and renderer manifests. Not for blind readers |
+    29	
+    30	Every generated file is rebuilt, byte for byte, by
+    31	`tools/build_literature_example.py` in the source repository.
+    32	
+    33	## Formats
+    34	
+    35	This section is the one home of the formats below. Every other mention cites it.
+    36	
+    37	### `literature-corpus/1`
+    38	
+    39	- **`model`, `model_sha256`:** the model file and its digest.
+    40	- **`artifacts`:** the `gnd extract` bundle path, plus the digests of its
+    41	  applicable set and projection.
+    42	- **`documents[]`:** each document's `id`, `path` and `sha256`.
+    43	  - `domain`: the document's field.
+    44	  - `split`: `dev` or `heldout`.
+    45	  - `paraphrase`: always `true`.
+    46	  - `work`: the work's `identifier`, `title` and `year`.
+    47	- **`passages[]`:** `id` (`<document>.p<n>`), `document` and `span` (`start`
+    48	  and `end`, half-open character offsets into the NFC text). Line 1 is the
+    49	  title and line 2 the identifier; every later line is one passage.
+    50	
+    51	### `literature-answer-key/1`
+    52	
+    53	- **`corpus_sha256`:** the SHA-256 of the `corpus.json` bytes.
+    54	- **`labeller`:** who wrote the labels. `independent` is `false` here.
+    55	- **`assertions[]`:** one passage-level claim each.
+    56	  - `passage`, `participants`, and `predicate` in plain words.
+    57	  - `support`: one of `supported`, `stretched`, `contradicted`,
+    58	    `insufficient-evidence`.
+    59	  - `fact`: the claim in the flat model.
+    60	  - `label_disagreement`: `null` when the labellers agreed.
+    61	- **`anchored_facts[]`:** every anchored role of a gold fact, with `source`,
+    62	  `fact`, `role`, `passage` and its gold `span`.
+    63	- **`populations[]`:** each source's `expected_check_exit` under plain
+    64	  `gnd check`.
+    65	- **`chains[]`:** labelled lineage paths, kept here for the lineage phase.
+    66	  - `label`: `supported` or `false`.
+    67	  - `story` and `domains` (`same` or `cross`).
+    68	  - `edges`: assertion ids.
+    69	  - `merges`: identity pair ids that a false merge needs.
+    70	  - `path`, `passages` and `reason`.
+    71	- **`reuse_cases[]`:** names reused across works. `kind` is one of `real`,
+    72	  `generic-word`, `homonym`.
+    73	- **`identity_pairs[]`:** cross-source key pairs, each a `left` and a `right`
+    74	  key (`source`, `object_type`, `value`). `label` is `same` or `different`.
+    75	- **`name_pairs[]`:** pairs of names. `kind` is one of `homonym`, `synonym`,
+    76	  `family`.
+    77	- **`heldout`:** the SHA-256 of the held-out answer key, and its chain counts.
+    78	  The held-out key itself is not in this branch.
+    79	
+    80	### `literature-run/1`
+    81	
+    82	```
+    83	run.json        {"schema": "literature-run/1", "corpus_sha256": "...",
+    84	                 "sources": [...], "abstentions": ["<passage id>", ...]}
+    85	composition/    a gnd compose directory: manifest.json, and per source
+    86	                document.txt, population.json, population.json.anchors.json,
+    87	                population.json.extraction.json
+    88	composed/       population.json and record.json written by gnd compose
+    89	```
+    90	
+    91	A run abstains on a passage by listing its id. A run may not anchor a fact
+    92	inside a passage it abstains on.
+    93	
+    94	### `literature-assurance-report/1`
+    95	
+    96	The report written by `python -m gnd.examples.literature.assurance_report
+    97	CORPUS ANSWERS RUN [--out PATH]`, where CORPUS is this directory.
+    98	
+    99	**Exit codes.** The process exits with the report's `exit_code`:
+   100	- 0 when every active stage is `pass`;
+   101	- 1 when any active stage is `fail`, or `not-measured` because the run left
+   102	  out a route's input;
+   103	- 3 when the harness refuses an input.
+   104	
+   105	**A refused input** gets `stages: []` and a `reason`. The reasons, in
+   106	validation order:
+   107	1. `input:unreadable`, `input:too-large` (every document is capped at 4 MiB
+   108	   on read), `input:not-json` (no NaN, depth at most 64, integers at most 128
+   109	   digits);
+   110	2. `input:schema`;
+   111	3. `corpus:digest-mismatch`, `run:corpus-mismatch`;
+   112	4. `run:unknown-source`, `run:document-mismatch`, `run:unknown-passage`,
+   113	   `run:abstained-passage-asserted`;
+   114	5. `internal`, for anything unforeseen.
+   115	
+   116	**Top-level fields:**
+   117	- `schema`, `exit_code` and `reason` (`null` when scored);
+   118	- `inputs`: `corpus_sha256`, `answers_sha256`, `run_sha256` (a digest over
+   119	  every file of the run), `gnd_version` and `harness_sha256`;
+   120	- `labels`: the answer key's labeller `kind`, `independent` and `note`;
+   121	- `stages`: the seven stages, in order;
+   122	- `failed_stages`, in stage order;
+   123	- `not_measured`: each stage not measured, with its owner;
+   124	- `unscored_labels`: the label groups this report does not score, with their
+   125	  counts and owners.
+   126	
+   127	**Fields of each active stage:**
+   128	- `status`: `pass`, `fail` or `not-measured`;
+   129	- `owner`: `null`, or `run` when the run left out the stage's route input;
+   130	- `failed_floors`: names from `agreement`, `precision`, `recall`,
+   131	  `abstention`, `refused` and `route-refused`;
+   132	- `unit`, `sample_size`, `exclusions` and `unresolved`;
+   133	- `disagreements`: `count` and `units`;
+   134	- `label_disagreements` and `refused_units`;
+   135	- `floor`: each floor as an exact fraction, where a `_max` key is an upper
+   136	  bound;
+   137	- `routes`: each route's `argv`, shown relative to the inputs, and its
+   138	  `exit`;
+   139	- `route_refused`, set when the stage's whole route refused;
+   140	- the metrics `agreement`, `precision`, `recall` and `abstention`, each with
+   141	  its `numerator`, `denominator` and `rate` (`null` when the denominator is
+   142	  0).
+   143	
+   144	`semantic-support` adds `per_domain` and an `aggregate` note.
