+{"role":"tool","tool_call_id":"tool_ICVoPT289w64mKRvrJEU82CD","content":"1\t# Literature reading reference · plan r3\n2\t\n3\t**Status: r3, 2026-11-23. Revised after the first panel round; not yet\n4\tapproved.** r2 was the full plan `start-milestone` wrote from outline r1\n5\t(2026-11-22, merged in PR 229). The owner delegated `auto=true` for this\n6\tmilestone on 2026-11-23 and selected the full GND panel for plan and close\n7\treview (D6). The first round read r2\n8\t([r2 panel](../../../reviews/literature-reading-reference-r2-panel/README.md));\n9\tr3 applies its seven fixes, listed in §11. Approval is recorded only in a\n10\tplan-gate record, which does not exist yet.\n11\t\n12\tThis plan covers one open issue,\n13\t[#226](https://github.com/vasiloglou/gnd-public/issues/226): a host reading\n14\tscientific papers with GND's literature stages measured about 30 % recall for\n15\tevery model it tried and has no reference point to tell its own loss from the\n16\tmethod's.\n17\t\n18\t**Provenance.** Phase IDs LR1–LR3 and decisions D1–D5 are the outline's. r2\n19\tadds criteria IDs, obligations H1–H22 and decisions D6–D9. The outline left\n20\ttwo rows \"not read\". Both are read now, and both change the plan `(r2)`:\n21\t\n22\t- **LR1.** `gnd verify --format json` already lists every failing constraint\n23\t  with its rows (H2). What is missing is a page that says so, the rows in the\n24\t  text output, and the constraint's declared id (H3, H4).\n25\t- **LR2.** The reading example has no quote and refuses none (H8). The 44\n26\t  claims #226 lost to \"quote outside the shown section\" were dropped by the\n27\t  reporter's own host. GND's one binding of a quote to text is the anchor\n28\t  check, against the whole document a sidecar names (H10).\n29\t\n30\tr3 adds H23–H25 and re-ran H2–H4 and H22 at `11a7ebf7` `(r3)`. Code\n31\tcitations were read at `548b9a1d` (`main`, 2026-11-23). `main` moved to\n32\t`78934ee3` the same day (PR 234, PR 236, PR 237) while this plan was written;\n33\tthose merges changed planning files and six skill scripts that dev-tools\n34\tdelivers, and nothing under `src`, `tests` or `docs`. H17 and §3 are brought\n35\tup to them `(m)`. Rows marked\n36\t\"agent\" were read by the read-only harvest agent of this session and not all\n37\tre-read by the author; the rows the plan rests on (H2, H3, H4, H8, H9, H10,\n38\tH13) were re-read or run by the author.\n39\t\n40\t## Goal and completion predicates\n41\t\n42\tA host that reads papers with GND's literature stages can tell whether a low\n43\trecall is its own loss or the method's, can see why a source fails\n44\tverification, and knows where a quote may come from.\n45\t\n46\t- **LR1.** For a source that `gnd verify` fails, a host lists the constraints\n47\t  that failed and the rows that violate each, from one documented place. A\n48\t  test holds the documented steps on a packaged failing source.\n49\t- **LR2.** The pages state one rule for where a quoted span may lie: inside\n50\t  the document the anchor sidecar names, by digest. They say what that means\n51\t  for a host that shows the model one section at a time, and a test holds\n52\t  both sides of the rule.\n53\t- **LR3.** A labelled set ships with the literature example. A recorded run\n54\t  of the reading stages over it reports recall and precision per stage\n55\t  setting, names the producer and settings, and replays with zero producer\n56\t  calls.\n57\t\n58\tThe milestone is closed when each phase has its record and the close record\n59\tcites green focused gates for LR1–LR3, the structure and inventory guards,\n60\t`uv run ruff check` `(r3)`, the component-index check, a drafted\n61\treply to #226, and a close review on the instrument D6 selects.\n62\t\n63\t## 0. Binding design rules\n64\t\n65\t- **R0.1 Formats stay.** The anchor sidecar and the default `gnd verify`\n66\t  payload and text keep their fields and bytes; #226 says they work as they\n67\t  are. Anything LR1 adds is opt-in or a page (D7).\n68\t- **R0.2 A reference figure is a measurement of one run.** It names the\n69\t  producer, the settings and the labelled set. It is not a claim about other\n70\t  corpora or models, and the page says so beside every figure.\n71\t- **R0.3 No fidelity rate in the `verify` payload.** `gnd verify` keeps\n72\t  saying that checker acceptance is not proof of faithfulness\n73\t  (`extraction-contract.md:152`); the reference figures live in the\n74\t  example's own record.\n75\t- **R0.4 Fixture bytes stay.** The packaged reading run, its pinned producer\n76\t  table and its recorded outputs are byte-identical after LR1 and LR2. LR3\n77\t  adds a second fixture beside them and changes none.\n78\t- **R0.5 The gate calls no model.** A live run happens once, under the\n79\t  owner's explicit selection of a producer and a budget (D3). Every gate\n80\t  replays recorded responses.\n81\t- **R0.6 One home per rule.** How to read a failed verification has one\n82\t  home (D8); the quote rule lives in `docs/reference/anchors.md`; the\n83\t  literature pages cite both.\n84\t\n85\tThe project's six standing rules\n86\t(`.codex/skills/process-phase/references/project.md`) and the rule \"General\n87\tsolutions, not pattern-matched ones\" (`AGENTS.md`) bind every phase; each\n88\tspec discharges them by name.\n89\t\n90\t## 1. Scope, sources and authority\n91\t\n92\tHarvested obligations. \"Observed\" means read or run by the author at\n93\t`548b9a1d` on 2026-11-23; \"agent\" means read by the harvest agent;\n94\t\"inherited\" means claimed by a source and not re-measured.\n95\t\n96\t| # | Source | Obligation | Kind | Phase |\n97\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n98\t| H1 | #226 request 3 | \"A way to see why `verify` reports `alethic-failure` per source: which constraint, on which rows.\" The reporter saw it on all 20 sources (3,729 rows, 280 of them unanchored) | inherited | LR1 |\n99\t| H2 | probe `(r3)`: run `python -m gnd.examples.literature.reading run --out RUN`, copy `reading_fixture/reading.orm.json` and its include `depth_fixture/vocabulary.orm.json` into `RUN`, then `gnd verify RUN/reading.orm.json RUN/reported-conflict.population.json --no-extraction --format json`. The include resolves beside the model file, so the vocabulary must sit there; `reading_fixture/` alone does not hold it | exit 1, `outcome: \"violated\"`, `schema_version: 7`, `counts.fail: 1`. The one failing verdict carries `constraint_ref` (`fact_types[subject_label].constraints[0]`), `constraint_type` (`uniqueness`), `modality` (`alethic`), `source`, `status`, a `message` that verbalizes the constraint with the violating value, and `offending_facts`, the two violating rows in full. Each verdict also carries `verdict` (`fail`) and `reason_code`; the report itself carries `outcome` and `counts` `(r3)` | observed (re-run at `11a7ebf7`) | LR1 |\n100\t| H3 | the same probe, text output | one line under `BLOCKING (alethic)`: `[fail] <constraint_ref>: <message>`. The rows are not printed; the value appears only inside the message | observed | LR1 |\n101\t| H4 | the same probe; `commands/checking.py:904` | the constraint's declared id (`subject_has_one_label`) is not in the payload. `gnd verify --rule-ids` is refused (\"unrecognized arguments\"); the flag exists on `gnd check` only | observed | LR1 (D7) |\n102\t| H5 | `redaction.py:616-633` | when a model's redaction policy selects a verdict's roles, `message` becomes a fixed marker and `offending_facts` one marker row. A host of such a model sees which constraint failed and not the rows | observed by reading | LR1 |\n103\t| H6 | `docs/guides/documents.md:62-72`; `extraction-contract.md:136-148` | the pages say \"Read the `extraction` block and the semantic verdicts independently\" and \"REPORT IT\" for exit 1. No page shows how to list the failing constraints and their rows from a verify report. `offending_facts` is shown on the expense and book tutorial pages, for the Python `check` call, and on no verify page | observed | LR1 |\n104\t| H7 | #226 request 2; \"What we measured\" | 82 claims dropped host-side: 44 quoted text outside the shown section, 38 used a participant or predicate not in the quote. \"Nearly half of our drops were quotes from elsewhere in the paper, and many looked correct\" | inherited | LR2 |\n105\t| H8 | `src/gnd/examples/literature/reading.py:176-193` | a producer response is `{outcome, facts, proposal_keys}`. There is no quote in it and no check of one. Each attempt records the shown section's span and `excerpt_sha256`, which binds the attempt to the section, not a claim to a quote | observed | LR2 |\n106\t| H9 | `src/gnd/examples/literature/reading.py:390-392` | the document the example hands to `gnd verify` is the attempted sections of one pass joined with newlines, not the declared source and not one shown section. The example writes no anchor sidecar, so it is not a model for anchoring `(r3)` | observed | LR2 |\n107\t| H10 | `anchors.py:238-271`; `docs/reference/anchors.md:41-45` | `bind_anchors` requires the sidecar's `document_sha256` to equal the digest of the document bytes, and each anchor's span to lie inside that document with a matching `excerpt_sha256`; otherwise `anchor:binding-mismatch`. `block_id` and `unit_id` are free labels. So a quote is bound to a document version, never to a section | observed | LR2 |\n108\t| H11 | `src/gnd/examples/literature/reading.py:93-112` | a roster section is `source`, `kind`, `span` (byte offsets, increasing per source) and `selection` (`opening` or `targeted`); an unavailable section has no span and a reason | observed | LR2, LR3 |\n109\t| H12 | #226 request 1 | \"A reference recall (and precision) for the literature-reading stages on a labelled set, with the stage settings that produced it\" | inherited | LR3 |\n110\t| H13 | `src/gnd/examples/literature/reading.py:42`, `:311-341`; `docs/literature/index.md:309-313` | the example's only recall figure is fidelity recall over two fact types (`CALIBRATION_FACTS`), 2 of 2 and 1 of 2, used to qualify one scoped absence. No precision is measured | observed | LR3 |\n111\t| H14 | #226 \"What we measured\" | on 20 papers and about 100 gold claims: broad pass best recall 29 of 101 with precision 15 to 38 %; targeted pass best recall 30 of 100 with precision 10 to 20 %; 233 kept claims outside the gold passages; 3 of 29 aligned claims agree on the predicate | inherited | LR3 (comparison rows) |\n112\t| H15 | `src/gnd/examples/literature/reading.py:149-158`, `:562-584` | the producer is duck-typed (`produce(stage, section)`); `run()` wires the saved producer; `replay()` re-derives from the retained table with zero producer calls and requires byte identity, for the packaged fixture only | observed | LR3 |\n113\t| H16 | `src/gnd/examples/literature/reading.py:587-596` | a run's settings are `--budget-bytes` and `--scenario`; broad against targeted is fixed per section in the roster. There is no other stage setting to report | observed | LR3 (D9) |\n114\t| H17 | [literature-host-interfaces](../../literature-host-interfaces/README.md) plan r6, approved by the owner on 2026-11-23 (PR 237) `(m)` | LH1: a public producer protocol in the reading example, a caller's producer driving the six stages over a caller's root. LH2: `replay()` of a live run from its own folder with zero producer calls, its settings read from the run. LH1 has a draft spec waiting for its owner gate; nothing is implemented | observed by reading | LR3 needs both |\n115\t| H18 | literature-depth close, `016-milestone-close.md:162` | \"Host-scale reading and extraction: carry to the existing literature-identity follow-up for a separately selected corpus and provider budget\" | observed by reading | LR3 (D1) |\n116\t| H19 | #226 \"Not requested\" | \"Changes to the anchor or verify formats. Those work for us as they are\" | inherited | R0.1 |\n117\t| H20 | `tests/test_verify_cli.py:309-336`, `:387` | exit 1 with `outcome: \"violated\"` and a deontic-only violation at exit 0 are pinned; every verify exit path validates against `report.schema.json`. No test lists failing constraints with their rows for `gnd verify` | agent | LR1 |\n118\t| H21 | `docs/literature/index.md`, `docs/literature/commands.md`; `src/gnd/examples/literature/README.md` | the literature pages. `index.md:11` says \"GND keeps each claim tied to its passage\"; none states where a quote may lie | agent, in part observed | LR2 |\n119\t| H22 `(r3)` | `project.md`, \"Test and gate commands\"; dev-tools re-sync `a29562a` | whole-repository `uv run ruff check` exits 0 at `11a7ebf7` and is the lint gate `project.md` names. r2's wording, retired: \"whole-repository `uv run ruff check` exits 1 on files dev-tools delivers; `uv run ruff check src tests tools` exits 0, and is this plan's lint gate\" | observed (re-run 2026-11-23 at `11a7ebf7`) | all |\n120\t| H23 `(r3)` | `src/gnd/checker/_reports.py:113-116`; `src/gnd/output.py:365` | exit 1 also follows from a failing derivation verdict. Derivation verdicts travel in a separate `derivations` array, rendered under `DERIVATIONS (alethic)` | observed by reading | LR1 |\n121\t| H24 `(r3)` | `src/gnd/examples/literature/README.md`; `src/gnd/examples/literature/assurance_report.py:490-531` | the package already ships a labelled literature regression corpus (synthetic paraphrases of public works; answer key `literature-answer-key/1` with passage, participants and predicate) and a staged scorer that reports precision and recall against it per stage (`source-binding`, `semantic-support`, and others) | observed | LR3 (D1, D4) |\n122\t| H25 `(r3)` | `tests/test_packaged_skills.py:101-125` | a relative link from a packaged skill file must resolve inside the packaged skills tree, so a skill page cites a `docs/` page by absolute URL | observed by reading | LR1 (D8) |\n123\t\n124\tComponent index (`dev/components/`), searched 2026-11-23:\n125\t\n126\t| Capability | Search | Found |\n127\t|---|---|---|\n128\t| verify a population | `search \"verify population\"` | `cli.verify_command`, `export.verification` |\n129\t| the reading example | `search \"literature reading\"` | `examples.literature_reading` (`reading.py`), `examples.literature_assurance` |\n130\t| bind a quote to text | `search \"anchor quote\"` | no component of its own; `src/gnd/anchors.py` is found by `git grep bind_anchors` |\n131\t| score a reading against labels | `search \"literature reading\"` | `assurance_report.py`: `score_reading_coverage` scores coverage; `_semantic` and `_source_binding` score precision and recall against the regression corpus's answer key (H24). r2's \"scores coverage, not recall against gold\" was wrong `(r3)` |\n132\t\n133\t`components_index.py review` lists 276 leads in 10 groups; an index review\n134\tis offered to the owner and is not a prerequisite.\n135\t\n136\t### Issue requests and the phases that discharge them\n137\t\n138\t| Request | Phase |\n139\t|---|---|\n140\t| #226: a way to see why `verify` fails a source, by constraint and row | LR1 |\n141\t| #226: guidance on whether a claim's quote must lie inside the shown section | LR2 |\n142\t| #226: a reference recall and precision on a labelled set, with the settings | LR3 |\n143\t\n144\tThis is a framework milestone. An experiment-claim census (`gnd coverage`)\n145\tcounts claims over a domain model; it does not measure a reader's recall or\n146\tsay what a report page explains, so none is read here. The baseline is rows\n147\tH2, H3, H13 and H14.\n148\t\n149\t## 2. Contract proposal and product behavior\n150\t\n151\t### LR1 — why a source fails verification\n152\t\n153\t- One page section, \"Reading a failed verification\" (home: D8), gives the\n154\t  steps: run `gnd verify … --format json` with the model's includes beside\n155\t  it (H2); the failing constraints are the `verdicts` with\n156\t  `verdict: \"fail\"` and `modality: \"alethic\"`, and the failing derivations\n157\t  are the `derivations` with `verdict: \"fail\"` (H23) `(r3)`; each names its\n158\t  constraint by `constraint_ref` and `constraint_type`, says what it\n159\t  requires in `message`, and lists the violating rows in\n160\t  `offending_facts`. A failing derivation names itself by `derivation_ref`\n161\t  and lists its `findings` `(r3)`. It says what a deontic violation is (a\n162\t  `warning`, exit unchanged) and what a redacting model hides (H5).\n163\t- A test runs those steps on the reading example's reported conflict, on a\n164\t  passing source, on a deontic-only violation, on a redacting model and on\n165\t  a derivation failure, and fails if the page names a field the report does\n166\t  not carry. The last three fixtures are authored in the test `(r3)`.\n167\t- Proposed under D7 (a): nothing in `gnd verify` changes. The alternatives,\n168\t  printing the rows in text and accepting `--rule-ids`, are the owner's.\n169\t- Details are in the [LR1 draft spec](phase-LR1-spec.md).\n170\t\n171\t### LR2 — where a quote may lie\n172\t\n173\tProposed under D2 (b):\n174\t\n175\t- `docs/reference/anchors.md` states the rule in one sentence: a span is\n176\t  bound to the document whose digest the sidecar names, and may lie anywhere\n177\t  in it. GND has no notion of the section a model was shown.\n178\t- The literature pages say what follows for a host. If the host verifies\n179\t  against the whole source version, a correct quote from another section\n180\t  binds, and the section it was shown in can be recorded in `block_id`. If\n181\t  the host verifies against the shown section only, a quote from elsewhere\n182\t  cannot bind. Which document a host names is the host's choice, and the\n183\t  pages recommend the whole source version.\n184\t- The packaged reading example is neither case: it verifies the attempted\n185\t  sections of one pass joined with newlines and writes no anchor sidecar\n186\t  (H9). Its README says so and says it is not a model for anchoring `(r3)`.\n187\t  r2 said the example verifies \"against the shown section only\", which was\n188\t  wrong.\n189\t- `docs/literature/index.md:11` (\"GND keeps each claim tied to its\n190\t  passage\") is rewritten to cite the anchor rule `(r3)`.\n191\t- A test pins both sides with one source and one quote from its second\n192\t  section: bound when the sidecar and the document are the whole source;\n193\t  refused with `anchor:binding-mismatch: source span or excerpt differs`\n194\t  when the sidecar's `document_sha256` names the first section and the\n195\t  document is that section, so the span rule refuses and not the digest\n196\t  check `(r3)`. The same test reads the pages for the rule's one statement\n197\t  and the citations.\n198\t- The reading example's code and fixture do not change (R0.4).\n199\t\n200\t### LR3 — a labelled reference set and one recorded run\n201\t\n202\t- A labelled set the example reads: either the packaged regression corpus\n203\t  and its answer key (H24), extended where the reading stages need\n204\t  sections, or a second fixture beside `reading_fixture/` (D1) `(r3)`.\n205\t- One live run over it through the producer protocol of LH1, recorded and\n206\t  replayed as LH2 specifies (D3). The record gives recall and precision per\n207\t  stage (broad, targeted) under the scoring rule of D4, and names the\n208\t  producer, the budget and the roster.\n209\t- The literature page shows the figures beside the scoring rule, the set's\n210\t  size and the sentence of R0.2, with #226's own figures (H14) as the row a\n211\t  host compares against.\n212\t\n213\t## 3. Dependencies and artifact homes\n214\t\n215\t| Phase | Needs | Homes it changes |\n216\t|---|---|---|\n217\t| LR1 | D7 and D8 ruled, or the plan gate accepting their proposals | the home D8 names (proposed `docs/guides/documents.md`), `src/gnd/skills/gnd-extract/references/extraction-contract.md` (one citing line, absolute URL, H25), `docs/literature/commands.md` (one citing line), a new `tests/test_lr1_verify_failure_listing.py`, `docs/release-notes.md` |\n218\t| LR2 | D2 ruled | `docs/reference/anchors.md`, `docs/literature/index.md`, `docs/literature/commands.md`, `src/gnd/examples/literature/README.md`, a new `tests/test_lr2_quote_scope.py`, `docs/release-notes.md` |\n219\t| LR3 | LH1 and LH2 of literature-host-interfaces implemented and merged; D1, D3, D4 and D9 ruled | a new fixture folder under `src/gnd/examples/literature/` or the regression corpus (D1), `reading.py` only if LH1 leaves a scoring hook or a settings field to add (D9) `(r3)`, the literature pages, a new `tests/test_lr3_reference_set.py`, the packaged-example inventories the guards name, `notes/issue-reply.md` |\n220\t\n221\tOrder: LR1 and LR2 in either order, LR3 last. The graph is acyclic. LR1 is\n222\tthe first ready phase: it needs no other milestone. LR3 is an outline until\n223\tthe code of LH1 and LH2 is on `main` and its rulings exist.\n224\t\n225\tOther writers. literature-host-interfaces changes\n226\t`src/gnd/examples/literature/reading.py` and the literature pages. One\n227\twriter at a time on `reading.py`: LR1 and LR2 do not touch it, and LR3\n228\tstarts after LH2 merges. LR1 and LR2 change `docs/literature/commands.md`,\n229\tand LR2 `index.md`, by a few lines `(r3)`; the second branch to merge rebases. Start\n230\tcondition: `main` at `78934ee3` or later.\n231\t\n232\tFixtures. LR1 uses the packaged reading run's reported conflict\n233\t(`reported-conflict.population.json`, which the example already verifies at\n234\texit 1) and its broad population (exit 0), staged with the model and its\n235\tincluded vocabulary as H2 says. It authors in the test a deontic-only\n236\tviolation (the case of `tests/test_verify_cli.py:309`), a redacting copy of\n237\tthe reading model, and a derivation failure `(r3)`. LR2 authors one two-section\n238\tsource and its sidecars in the test. LR3's fixture is its own deliverable.\n239\t\n240\t## 4. Phases\n241\t\n242\t| Phase | Criteria | Inherited residuals |\n243\t|---|---|---|\n244\t| LR1 | **LR1-C1** on the reported conflict, the documented steps give exactly one failing constraint, `fact_types[subject_label].constraints[0]`, kind `uniqueness`, with its two rows. **LR1-C2** on the broad population they give none, exit 0. **LR1-C3** every field name the page's section uses, at report level or verdict level, is a property in `report.schema.json` and a key of the live report at that level; the test takes the names from the section's code spans `(r3)`. **LR1-C4** a deontic-only violation is listed as a warning and not as a failure; a redacting model gives the marker and the page says so. **LR1-C5** the default `gnd verify` text and JSON for both sources are byte-identical before and after the phase (R0.1). **LR1-C6** `(r3)` every exit-1 fixture gives a non-empty listing, including a derivation failure | none |\n245\t| LR2 | **LR2-C1** the rule is stated once, in `anchors.md`, and the literature pages cite it. **LR2-C2** a quote from a source's second section binds when the sidecar names the whole source. **LR2-C3** the same quote is refused with `anchor:binding-mismatch: source span or excerpt differs` when the sidecar's document is the first section alone `(r3)`. **LR2-C4** the packaged reading run replays byte-identical (R0.4). **LR2-C5** `(r3)` the example's README says its verify document is the joined attempted sections with no sidecar, and `index.md:11` cites the rule; the test reads both | none |\n246\t| LR3 | **LR3-C1** the labelled set ships with a licence per source and a count of gold facts per fact type. **LR3-C2** replay of the recorded run makes zero producer calls and reproduces the figures byte for byte. **LR3-C3** the record gives recall and precision per stage, with numerator and denominator, and names the producer, budget and roster. **LR3-C4** a changed gold fact, a changed response and a changed section each change the figure or refuse; the scoring rule has one fixture that tells \"found\" from \"found with the wrong predicate\". **LR3-C5** the page shows the figures, the scoring rule and R0.2's sentence, beside #226's figures. **LR3-C6** the reply to #226 is drafted and names what is on `main` and what is not released | H18 |\n247\t\n248\tEach phase also runs the structure and inventory guards,\n249\t`uv run ruff check` `(r3)`, an independent review of its diff that\n250\tcarries the pattern-matched-solutions claim of `project.md`, a refactoring\n251\treview and its record.\n252\t\n253\t## 5. Documentation, skill and compatibility surfaces\n254\t\n255\t- **One home each (R0.6).** The verification steps live in the home D8\n256\t  names; `extraction-contract.md` §5 and the literature commands page cite\n257\t  it. The quote rule lives in `docs/reference/anchors.md`.\n258\t- **Packaged skills.** `gnd-extract`'s `extraction-contract.md` gains one\n259\t  citing line, an absolute URL to the docs home (H25) `(r3)`; its manifest is held by `tests/test_packaged_skills.py`, in\n260\t  the guard block.\n261\t- **Release notes.** One entry per phase under the unreleased heading.\n262\t- **Compatibility.** LR1 and LR2 change pages and tests only, under D7 (a)\n263\t  and D2 (b). D7 (c) would add an opt-in flag and keeps the default bytes.\n264\t  D7 (b) as written changes the default text, so ruling it would amend R0.1\n265\t  and LR1-C5 at the same gate `(r3)`. r2 said \"the default bytes stay either\n266\t  way\", which was wrong for (b). LR3 adds a\n267\t  fixture and changes none.\n268\t- **Issue reply.** The close drafts it; the owner posts it.\n269\t\n270\t## 6. Risks and required controls\n271\t\n272\t| Risk | Control |\n273\t|---|---|\n274\t| The page describes fields the report does not carry, or stops matching it | LR1-C3: the test reads the page's field names and checks each against the schema and a live report |\n275\t| The documented steps are fitted to one fixture | LR1-C1, C2 and C4 cover a failure, a pass, a deontic warning and a redacted verdict; the diff review's pattern-matched claim |\n276\t| A host reads