_group_cleaned":true,"return_code":0,"signal":null,"spawn_error":null,"spawned":true,"started_monotonic":4071398.170955625,"status":"succeeded","stderr":{"observed_bytes":0,"path":"reviewers/claude/stderr","retained_bytes":0,"sha256":"e3b0c44298fc1c149afbf4c8996fb92427ae41e4649b934ca495991b7852b855","truncated":false},"stdin_sha256":"5da44852bc0b79f0d281b2126ff445aefd922acf4951ddbc69b28fd467bc39d1","stdout":{"observed_bytes":13304,"path":"reviewers/claude/stdout","retained_bytes":13304,"sha256":"c1959689b462fb0730f6ad0cd5968a3ccab81c7eb9c4f9e34ecb2869e965ac8f","truncated":false},"termination":"exited","termination_steps":[],"timed_out":false},"issues":[],"response":{"artifact_reads":[{"digest_matched":true,"path":".gnd-planning/milestones/literature-reading-reference/notes/literature-reading-reference-plan.md"}],"concerns":[{"concern_id":"lr1-redaction-disclosure-mode","evidence":[{"artifact_path":".gnd-planning/milestones/literature-reading-reference/notes/literature-reading-reference-plan.md","location":"§1 H5, line 102","observation":"H5 reads \"when a model's redaction policy selects a verdict's roles, `message` becomes a fixed marker and `offending_facts` one marker row. A host of such a model sees which constraint failed and not the rows\". At this checkout the disclosure mode decides redaction. docs/reference/disclosure.md:20 gives the default as \"| unset | redacted unless `--unredacted` |\". Line 210 lists `gnd verify` as \"declared roles: shown under `internal`, redacted under `customer`\". src/gnd/commands/verify.py:66 calls `_add_unredacted_argument(verify_parser)`. So outside `customer` mode a host of a redacting model can get the rows. docs/how-to/disclosure-mode.md already documents this, and it shows `offending_facts`, although H6 does not count that page."},{"artifact_path":".gnd-planning/milestones/literature-reading-reference/notes/literature-reading-reference-plan.md","location":"§2 LR1, line 161; §0 R0.6, line 81","observation":"§2 LR1 has the page say \"what a redacting model hides (H5)\", and R0.6 reads \"**R0.6 One home per rule.**\". Neither the LR1 steps nor the §5 citations name docs/reference/disclosure.md, `--unredacted`, `--disclosure internal` or `GND_DISCLOSURE`. The page would therefore restate a rule that already has a home, and would not tell a host how to get the rows it is allowed to see."},{"artifact_path":".gnd-planning/milestones/literature-reading-reference/notes/literature-reading-reference-plan.md","location":"§3 Fixtures, lines 236-237; H2, line 99","observation":"The plan authors \"a redacting copy of\" / \"the reading model, and a derivation failure `(r3)`\", and H2 copies \"`reading_fixture/reading.orm.json` and its include `depth_fixture/vocabulary.orm.json`\". The failing constraint `fact_types[subject_label].constraints[0]` is declared in vocabulary.orm.json, which reading.orm.json includes under a pinned sha256 (`25ac1974…`). src/gnd/redaction.py:97-106 reads `redact: true` from the fact type or role itself. A copy that edits only reading.orm.json therefore does not redact this verdict. The result also depends on the test process's GND_DISCLOSURE, which the plan does not pin."}],"recommendation":"Extend H5 to cover the disclosure modes. Have the LR1 page cite docs/reference/disclosure.md and say that `--unredacted` or `--disclosure internal` shows the rows outside `customer` mode. Specify the redacting fixture as a vocabulary copy with `redact: true` on subject_label and a re-pinned include digest. Run the test with GND_DISCLOSURE unset and once under `internal`.","relevance":"hardening","severity":"medium","title":"LR1's redaction case leaves out the disclosure mode that decides whether a host sees the rows"},{"concern_id":"lr3-corpus-extend-vs-r04","evidence":[{"artifact_path":".gnd-planning/milestones/literature-reading-reference/notes/literature-reading-reference-plan.md","location":"§10 D1, line 347; §2 LR3, line 203","observation":"D1 reads \"(d) `(r3)` Reuse or extend the packaged regression corpus and its answer key (H24)\", and §2 LR3 reads \"and its answer key (H24), extended where the reading stages need\"."},{"artifact_path":".gnd-planning/milestones/literature-reading-reference/notes/literature-reading-reference-plan.md","location":"§0 R0.4, line 77; §5, lines 266-267","observation":"R0.4 reads \"adds a second fixture beside them and changes none.\" and §5 ends \"LR3 adds a\" / \"fixture and changes none.\". Extending the corpus changes an existing pinned fixture. tests/test_li1_assurance_report.py:7 says the expected outcomes \"live in src/gnd/examples/literature/expected/ and were approved with the corpus\", and src/gnd/examples/literature/answers/heldout.sha256 pins a held-out digest."},{"artifact_path":".gnd-planning/milestones/literature-reading-reference/notes/literature-reading-reference-plan.md","location":"§9 LR3 lane, line 334","observation":"The LR3 lane is \"`uv run pytest -q tests/test_lr3_reference_set.py tests/test_ld3_reading_workflow.py tests/test_ld4_revision.py tests/test_ld5_verdict.py --tb=short`\". It leaves out tests/test_li1_assurance_report.py, tests/test_li3_diagnostics.py, tests/test_li4_assertions.py and tests/test_release_acceptance.py. All four reference the assurance scorer, the answer key or the regression corpus."}],"recommendation":"Choose one. Either limit D1 (d) to reuse (a copy, or an additive overlay that leaves corpus/, answers/ and expected/ byte-identical), or amend R0.4 and §5 to allow a corpus change and add the li1, li3, li4 and release-acceptance tests to the LR3 lane. Record the choice under fix-lr3-existing-corpus in §11.","relevance":"hardening","severity":"medium","title":"D1 (d) 'extend the regression corpus' contradicts R0.4 and §5, and the LR3 lane leaves out the corpus's tests"},{"concern_id":"lr2-example-sidecar-wording","evidence":[{"artifact_path":".gnd-planning/milestones/literature-reading-reference/notes/literature-reading-reference-plan.md","location":"§4 LR2-C5, line 245; §11, line 374","observation":"LR2-C5 reads \"the example's README says its verify document is the joined attempted sections with no sidecar\", and §11 reads \"the example verifies the joined attempted sections with no sidecar\". In fact `_checked` (src/gnd/examples/literature/reading.py:274-286) writes `<stem>.population.extraction.json`, built by `_extraction_sidecar` (:258-265), which carries `document` and `document_sha256` for that document. It then verifies with `--artifacts bundle`. The example writes an extraction sidecar that binds the document by digest. It writes no anchor sidecar."},{"artifact_path":".gnd-planning/milestones/literature-reading-reference/notes/literature-reading-reference-plan.md","location":"§1 H9, line 106","observation":"H9 reads \"the document the example hands to `gnd verify` is the attempted sections of one pass joined with newlines, not the declared source and not one shown section\". That is true for the broad and targeted passes (reading.py:391). For the reported conflict, which §3 makes LR1's main fixture, the document is `inputs.raw[\"conflict.txt\"]` (reading.py:395)."}],"recommendation":"Make LR2-C5 and the README say 'no anchor sidecar' and state that the extraction sidecar binds the joined document by digest. Note that the reported conflict is verified against conflict.txt. The LR2 test then holds an accurate sentence.","relevance":"hardening","severity":"low","title":"LR2-C5 would have the README say 'no sidecar', though the example writes an extraction sidecar"},{"concern_id":"lr1-c3-derivation-level","evidence":[{"artifact_path":".gnd-planning/milestones/literature-reading-reference/notes/literature-reading-reference-plan.md","location":"§4 LR1-C3, line 244","observation":"LR1-C3 reads \"**LR1-C3** every field name the page's section uses, at report level or verdict level, is a property in `report.schema.json` and a key of the live report at that level; the test takes the names from the section's code spans `(r3)`\"."},{"artifact_path":".gnd-planning/milestones/literature-reading-reference/notes/literature-reading-reference-plan.md","location":"§2 LR1, lines 157 and 160","observation":"r3 adds \"are the `derivations` with `verdict: \"fail\"` (H23)\" and \"A failing derivation names itself by `derivation_ref`\", followed by `findings`. In src/gnd/report.schema.json, `derivation_ref` and `findings` are properties of the `derivation_verdict*` definitions, not the constraint `verdict*` definitions (`verdict_v10` holds constraint_ref, offending_facts and the rest). The two packaged sources have no failing derivation. So 'verdict level' and 'the live report' do not say where these names are checked. The code spans that §2 models the section on also hold values (`\"fail\"`, `warning`, `uniqueness`), which are not field names."}],"recommendation":"State LR1-C3 over three levels: report, constraint verdict and derivation verdict. Check each against its schema definition and against the live report of the fixture that carries it; derivation fields come from the authored derivation failure. Say how the test separates field names from values in code spans, for example with a listed set of names or a marked syntax.","relevance":"hardening","severity":"low","title":"LR1-C3 does not cover the derivation-verdict fields r3 adds to the page"},{"concern_id":"issue-reply-timing-split","evidence":[{"artifact_path":".gnd-planning/milestones/literature-reading-reference/notes/literature-reading-reference-plan.md","location":"§10 D5, line 351","observation":"D5 adds \"`(r3)` An interim reply to #226 covering requests 3 and 2 is drafted when LR2 merges (`notes/issue-reply.md`); the owner posts it.\""},{"artifact_path":".gnd-planning/milestones/literature-reading-reference/notes/literature-reading-reference-plan.md","location":"§5, line 268; §3 LR3 row, line 219; §11, line 378","observation":"§5 still reads \"- **Issue reply.** The close drafts it; the owner posts it.\". §3 lists `notes/issue-reply.md` only among LR3's homes. §11 gives the fix's sites as \"component row, H24, §2 LR3, §3, D1, D4, D5, D9\", without §5. No LR1 or LR2 criterion, home or §8 step drafts the interim reply."}],"recommendation":"In §5, name both drafts: the interim reply when LR2 merges and the final reply at close. Add `notes/issue-reply.md` to LR2's row in §3, or to the LR1/LR2 pull-request step in §8. Add §5 to the fix's sites in §11.","relevance":"completeness","severity":"low","title":"D5's interim #226 reply has no home or step elsewhere, and §5 still says the close drafts it"},{"concern_id":"plan-gate-owner-vs-delegation","evidence":[{"artifact_path":".gnd-planning/milestones/literature-reading-reference/notes/literature-reading-reference-plan.md","location":"§8, lines 306-307","observation":"The plan reads \"plan gate, each spec gate and the close are ruled under that delegation\" and \"and recorded as delegated rulings. It authorizes no paid producer call,\"."},{"artifact_path":".gnd-planning/milestones/literature-reading-reference/notes/literature-reading-reference-plan.md","location":"§8, line 316; §10, line 343; §10 D3, line 349","observation":"The plan reads \"- The plan gate is recorded in `001-plan-gate.md` once the owner rules D1–D9\" and \"All rows are proposals until the plan gate rules them.\". Line 316 waits for the owner to rule D1–D9, while the auto=true delegation quoted in §8 lets the plan gate be ruled under delegation. D6 is already the owner's ruling. D3 (\"Owner names one model and a ceiling; the run is recorded once and replayed after\") cannot be ruled under a delegation that authorizes no paid producer call."}],"recommendation":"Reword line 316. Under the delegation, the plan gate rules D1, D2, D4, D5, D7, D8 and D9. It records D6 as the owner's ruling. It leaves D3 to the owner at LR3's spec gate (R0.5). LR1 and LR2 then do not wait for an owner ruling that the delegation already covers.","relevance":"completeness","severity":"low","title":"§8 has the plan gate wait for the owner to rule D1–D9, which conflicts with the auto=true delegation"}],"disposition":"revise","reviewer_id":"claude","schema_version":2,"summary":"No blocking concern. LR1 and LR2 can be specified, implemented and tested as sections 2 and 4 write them, with the section 9 lanes (all named test files exist). LR3's gate is clear: it starts only once LH1 and LH2 are on main and D1, D3, D4 and D9 are ruled. r3 resolves all seven first-round fixes at the sites section 11 names. fix-lr2-document-scope: H9 and section 2 LR2 are corrected, and LR2-C3 now asserts the anchors.py:270 span reason, which is reached only after the :249 digest check passes. fix-gates: whole-repository ruff is the gate in Goal, H22, section 4 and section 9, which agrees with project.md:167. fix-d7b-opt-in: corrected in section 5. fix-paths: H8 to H21 now carry the src/gnd prefix. fix-lr3-existing-corpus: the component row, H24, D1, D4 and D9 are updated. fix-lr1-steps: H2 gives the exact command, and H23, H25, LR1-C3 and LR1-C6 are added. fix-lr2-pages: index.md:11 and LR2-C5. Some fixes left small new inconsistencies, reported below: LR2-C5 says 'no sidecar', D1 (d) 'extend' conflicts with R0.4, LR1-C3 does not cover derivation fields, and section 5 conflicts with D5. The main hardening gap: LR1 leaves out the disclosure mode, which decides whether a host of a redacting model sees the rows. Every code citation I read matches this checkout: reading.py 42, 93-112, 149-158, 176-193, 311-341, 390-392 and 562-596; anchors.py 238-271; anchors.md 41-45; redaction.py 616-633; documents.md 62-72; extraction-contract.md 136-152; checking.py 904; _reports.py 113-116; output.py 365; assurance_report.py 490-531; test_packaged_skills.py 101-125; test_verify_cli.py 309 and 387; literature index.md 11 and 309-313; depth close 162; project.md 25 and 167. I did not re-run the H2 to H4 or H22 probes, as instructed."},"response_salvage":{"complete":true,"declared_concern_count":6,"dropped":[],"dropp